          {
            "document_hash": "2298b19af5aaee3bf3a21e7dde4eca0dcbe26162eab7916d459e4f42c0e5d184",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-2298b19af5aa-0002-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 41,
              "start": 40
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The central legal issue is whether the Sixth Circuit\u2019s exhaustion requirement, which demands that prisoners name or identify officials in administrative grievances, creates a heightened pleading standard that conflicts with Rule\u202f8 of the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "2298b19af5aaee3bf3a21e7dde4eca0dcbe26162eab7916d459e4f42c0e5d184",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-2298b19af5aa-0000-0002"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 4,
              "start": 4
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Justice Kennedy asked: Can a district court, sua sponte under Rule\u202f12(e), order a more definite statement when many claims are unexhausted?"
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Alito",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "2298b19af5aaee3bf3a21e7dde4eca0dcbe26162eab7916d459e4f42c0e5d184",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-2298b19af5aa-0000-0003"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 6,
              "start": 6
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Justice Alito, justice Alito questioned: Is it improper for a district court to dismiss a case at the screening stage when a prisoner\u2019s form reveals a claim was not exhausted?"
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "2298b19af5aaee3bf3a21e7dde4eca0dcbe26162eab7916d459e4f42c0e5d184",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-2298b19af5aa-0001-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 20,
              "start": 20
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Justice Ginsburg asked: If the exhaustion requirement is satisfied, why should the Court address what would happen if it were not?"
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "2298b19af5aaee3bf3a21e7dde4eca0dcbe26162eab7916d459e4f42c0e5d184",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-2298b19af5aa-0002-0002"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 41,
              "start": 40
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Justice Ginsburg also asked: What is the purpose of requiring the prisoner to name the individuals who made the decision in the administrative proceeding before suing them?"
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "2298b19af5aaee3bf3a21e7dde4eca0dcbe26162eab7916d459e4f42c0e5d184",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-2298b19af5aa-0001-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 22,
              "start": 22
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Justice Scalia asked: Would there be a problem if the state simply required naming the individuals?"
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Souter",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "2298b19af5aaee3bf3a21e7dde4eca0dcbe26162eab7916d459e4f42c0e5d184",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-2298b19af5aa-0002-0004"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 44,
              "start": 42
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Justice Souter asked: What justification exists for requiring the prisoner to name a party in the first administrative step?"
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "2298b19af5aaee3bf3a21e7dde4eca0dcbe26162eab7916d459e4f42c0e5d184",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-2298b19af5aa-0002-0005"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 48,
              "start": 47
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Justice Ginsburg, justice Ginsburg clarified: The rule is not an absolute policy that bars naming a person later if not named in the first step."
      }
    ],
